  "worked_solution": "Notice that $A$ is the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}.$\n\nSince we're multiplying the $3\\times 3$ matrix $B$ by the $2$nd *row* of the identity matrix ${I}_{3}$ on the *left*, the result must be the $2$nd *row* of $B.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nAB=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nLet's check the long way: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & -\\sqrt{7} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{5} & -3.2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
